2026-09-23T17:23:48.8526342Z     The ``pytest`` framework makes it easy to write small tests, yet
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8526851Z     scales to support complex functional testing for applications and libraries.
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8527257Z     
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8527467Z     An example of a simple test:
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8527731Z     
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8527936Z     .. code-block:: python
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8528181Z     
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8528397Z         # content of test_sample.py
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8528667Z         def inc(x):
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8528900Z             return x + 1
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8529130Z     
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8529320Z     
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8529519Z         def test_answer():
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8529760Z             assert inc(3) == 5
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8530004Z     
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8530177Z     
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8530362Z     To execute it::
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8530580Z     
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8530774Z         $ pytest
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8531063Z         ============================= test session starts =============================
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8531433Z         collected 1 items
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8531668Z     
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8531869Z         test_sample.py F
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8532102Z     
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8532349Z         ================================== FAILURES ===================================
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8532776Z         _________________________________ test_answer _________________________________
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8533135Z     
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8533332Z             def test_answer():
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8533597Z         >       assert inc(3) == 5
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8533866Z         E       assert 4 == 5
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8534122Z         E        +  where 4 = inc(3)
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8534379Z     
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8534597Z         test_sample.py:5: AssertionError
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8535078Z         ========================== 1 failed in 0.04 seconds ===========================
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8535427Z     
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8535611Z     
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8536611Z     Thanks to ``pytest``'s detailed assertion introspection, you can simply use plain ``assert`` statements. See `getting-started <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/getting-started.html#our-first-test-run>`_ for more examples.
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8537561Z     
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8537748Z     
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8537936Z     Features
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8538144Z     --------
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8538474Z     
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8539067Z     - Detailed info on failing `assert statements <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/assert.html>`_ (no need to remember ``self.assert*`` names)
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8539736Z     
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8539935Z     - `Auto-discovery
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8540379Z       <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/explanation/goodpractices.html#python-test-discovery>`_
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8540887Z       of test modules and functions
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8541151Z     
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8541534Z     - `Modular fixtures <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/explanation/fixtures.html>`_ for
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8542084Z       managing small or parametrized long-lived test resources
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8542432Z     
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8542805Z     - Can run `unittest <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/unittest.html>`_ (or trial)
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8543284Z       test suites out of the box
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8543540Z     
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8543753Z     - Python 3.10+ or PyPy3
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8544003Z     
2026-09-23T17:23:48.8544599Z     - Rich plugin architecture, with over 1300+ `external plugins <https://docs.pytest.org/en/latest/reference/plugin_list.html>`_ and thriving community
